  "conclusions": [
    "Both independent arms produce six checked derived-mask pairs. ROI loses the pink/red checker and printed checker while gaining two misleading results from bad crops.",
    "The predeclared fallback appears to raise derived-mask count from six to eight, but both additions visibly crop the wrong region. No verified source-reconstruction gain is demonstrated.",
    "The existing supportedCropOutline predicate rejects both false additions; requiring that predicate in diagnostic reporting leaves production and fallback at the same count. This is not a new application gate and does not disable user exports.",
    "The bounding rectangles generally contain the selected heart, but often include hands, handles, neighbouring objects or decorative backgrounds. Cropping ambiguity and RGB material/outline inference remain unresolved.",
    "Using original RGB preserves checker corners substantially better than replacing RGB with the learned material map. This does not by itself make the ROI approach sufficiently reliable for integration.",
    "The sloth fit is cleaner in the ROI arm, but the crop changes and timed local fitting both vary. This is not isolated evidence of a general fitting improvement.",
    "Bicycle detail is present in the classified mask and lost in both fitted outputs. Publisher verification subsequently establishes closed cut-outs in both bicycle and sloth ground-truth templates, outside the regular-slit representation. These are not ordinary ground-truth slit-recovery benchmarks. Painted-paper specks cause feature rejection, and printed-heart ink is treated as physical material by both RGB arms."
  ],
